## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of integration)
## DBsection(Work)
## KEYWORDS('definite integral', 'work')
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Author(Brian Burns)
## MLT(work_chain_gang)
## Level(5)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('6.6')
## Problem1('21')
DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextFraction.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$a = 0;
$B = random(50, 150, 10);
$c = random(5,  25,  1);

$f = Formula("$c*x")->reduce;
($D, $N) = reduce($c, 2);
$H = Formula("$D*x^2/$N")->reduce;
Context("Fraction");
$C2 = Compute("($B)**2*$c/2");

BEGIN_PGML

A [$B] ft length of steel chain weighing [$c] lb/ft is dangling from a pulley. How much work is required to wind the chain onto the pulley?
  
Work Done [`=`][_]{$C2} [`\;\textrm{lb/ft}`]

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Introduce an [`x`] -axis in the direction of the chain and with the origin at one end. Take a piece of the chain [`x`] feet from the end and of length [`dx`] feet. The work in moving this small segment of the chain a distance of [`x`] feet is
[``` F \cdot x```]
where the force required to lift this segment is  [`F(x)=[$c] dx`] pounds

Hence, the total work done is given by
 [```W=\int_{0}^{[$B]}  [$f] \;dx =[$C1]\left[[$H]\right]_{0}^{[$B]}=[$C2]```]

The Work Done is [`[$C2]`] ft[`\cdot`]lb.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
